course: biology
lesson: 136
title: Kung Fu Panda: real tigers and cranes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Tigress and Crane are fighters in the film, and their real animals are just as amazing: a tiger that loves a swim and a bird that dances in the snow.
explain: A bright orange tiger seems easy to spot, but deer cannot tell orange from green, so to them the tiger in the grass looks like dull green leaves with shadows.
explain: Walk behind a tiger and you will see a white spot on the back of each black ear, which may help cubs follow their mother through tall grass.
explain: Look up in winter and you may see cranes flying with their necks stretched straight out and hear their trumpet calls from kilometres away, made by a windpipe coiled like a trumpet's tube.
fact: Deer and wild pigs cannot tell red and orange from green, so to them a tiger's orange coat with black stripes looks dull green and blends into grass and shadows.
fact: Tiger stripes are on the skin as well as the fur, so a shaved tiger would still look striped.
fact: The South China tiger is a real kind of tiger, but none have been seen in the wild for decades; it survives only in zoos and breeding centres after its forests were cut and many were hunted.
fact: Tigers love water: they bathe on hot days, swim strongly, chase prey across rivers and in the Sundarbans mangroves of India and Bangladesh swim between islands.
fact: Tigers have bright white spots on the backs of their black ears, which may help cubs follow their mother and send signals.
fact: Tigers greet each other with a friendly puffing sound called chuffing, and they cannot purr like a house cat.
fact: Wild tiger numbers have risen in countries such as India and Nepal, to around 5,500 worldwide by recent counts, as was reported, thanks to guarding forests, stopping poachers and protecting prey.
fact: A tiger needs a large territory, so saving tigers also saves big areas of forest and all the animals in it.
fact: The red-crowned crane, a symbol of good luck and long life in China and Japan, stands about 1.5 metres tall, and the red patch on its head is bare skin that gets brighter when it is excited.
fact: Long legs let cranes wade in marshes and tall grass, and a long bill probes the mud for food.
fact: Cranes really dance, leaping, bowing, flapping and tossing sticks, which may help pairs stay together for many years; people travel to Hokkaido in Japan in winter to watch.
fact: A crane's windpipe is very long and coiled inside its breastbone like a trumpet's tube, so its call is loud and deep and carries several kilometres.
fact: Cranes fly with necks stretched out straight, while herons fly with necks folded back in an S; common cranes, gone from Britain for about 400 years, nest there again after wetlands were restored.
fact: Cranes, herons and flamingos often rest on one leg, which loses less heat from a leg tucked into warm feathers and may take little effort.
exercise: Why is an orange tiger hard for a deer to see? | Deer cannot tell orange from green, so it looks like dull green leaves | Tigers turn their fur green when they hunt, just the way a chameleon does | Deer are completely blind | Tigers hide only at night
exercise: What would a shaved tiger look like? | Still striped, because the stripes are on its skin too | Plain orange all over, because the stripes are only in the fur | Plain white all over | Spotted like a leopard
exercise: Where can you find South China tigers today? | Only in zoos and breeding centres | All over the forests of China | In the Arctic snow | In the jungles of Africa
exercise: Why did the South China tiger vanish from the wild? | Its forests were cut down and many were hunted | It moved away to India by itself to find more deer | It died of the cold | It turned into a snow leopard
exercise: Do tigers like water? | Yes, they bathe and are strong swimmers | No, they hate water like many pet cats | Only baby tigers swim | No, water makes their stripes fade
exercise: Where do tigers swim between islands in tidal rivers? | In the Sundarbans mangroves of India and Bangladesh | In the hot, dry sand dunes in the middle of the Sahara desert | On the Great Barrier Reef | In the Swiss Alps
exercise: Where are a tiger's white spots? | Behind its ears | On the tip of its nose, like a dot | Under its front paws | On its belly only
exercise: How may a tiger's white ear spots help cubs? | They help cubs follow their mother | They help the cubs see better at noon | They keep the cubs' ears warm and dry on cold winter nights | They scare away cubs from other mothers
exercise: How do tigers greet each other in a friendly way? | With a soft puffing sound called chuffing | With a loud purr | By barking twice | By roaring very loudly at each other in the forest
exercise: About how many wild tigers are there by recent counts? | Around 5,500 | Only around 55 | Around 550,000 | None left at all
exercise: How did tiger numbers rise in India and Nepal? | Forests were guarded, poachers stopped and prey protected | Tigers were brought in from zoos in Africa and set free in the forest | Tigers started eating plants | People fed them every day
exercise: About how tall is a red-crowned crane? | About 1.5 metres | About 5 metres, as tall as a grown-up giraffe | About 3 metres, like an elephant | About 50 centimetres, like a duck
exercise: What is the red patch on a red-crowned crane's head? | Bare red skin | A tuft of bright red feathers it grows in spring | Paint from the berries it eats | A small red horn
exercise: Why do cranes dance? | It may help pairs stay together for many years | To keep their feet from freezing | To catch fish with their wings | They only dance in films
exercise: Why can a crane's call be heard so far? | Its long windpipe is coiled like a trumpet's tube | It has two beaks | It shouts through a long hollow bill, like a megaphone | It calls only when the wind blows
exercise: How can you tell a flying crane from a flying heron? | A crane's neck is straight, a heron's is folded in an S | A crane flies upside down when the wind is strong | A heron has no neck | Only cranes flap their wings; herons glide without flapping
exercise: Why do cranes often stand on one leg? | A tucked-up leg loses less heat | The other leg is always broken | They are practising kung fu | To look taller to other cranes
exercise: Why does a tiger need protecting forests as well? | It needs a huge territory with plenty of prey | It sleeps up in a different tall tree each night of the year | It eats tree bark | It needs forests only for shade
test: «3 tigers walk off in the zoo, how many white ear spots can I see» | 6 | 3 | 12 | 9
test: «5 cranes on one leg on the frozen pond, how many feet touching the ice» | 5 | 10 | 0 | 15
test: «tigers hate water like my cat, everyone knows» What is right? | Tigers love water | Yes, tigers never go near water, just like my cat | Yes, water hurts their stripes | Only white tigers swim
test: In Winnie the Pooh, Tigger bounces on his springy tail all day. What can a real tiger do? | Swim strongly and stalk prey, but not bounce on its tail | Bounce on its tail like a spring, just as Tigger does all day | Fly short distances | Climb walls like a gecko
test: In Kung Fu Panda, Tigress purrs when she is happy. What does a real tiger do instead? | It chuffs, a soft puffing greeting; tigers cannot purr like house cats | It purrs much louder than a house cat, loud enough to hear across the forest | It barks like a dog | It sings like a bird
test: «bright orange tiger in green grass, stupidest camo ever» What is right? | Deer cannot tell orange from green, so to them it blends in | Tigers only hunt animals that are blind, so colour does not matter | Right, tigers never catch anything | Tigers turn green in the grass
test: «saw a big grey bird with its neck tucked in an S, that was a crane yeah?» What is right? | Probably a heron; cranes fly with necks stretched out straight | Yes, cranes always fold their necks in an S when they fly about | Yes, every grey bird is a crane | No, it was a flamingo
test: «why's the crane's red head going brighter» What is right? | The red is bare skin that gets brighter when it is excited | It is blushing from the cold wind, just like a person's cheeks | It has eaten too many berries | It has hurt its head
test: What would happen if every forest in a tiger's huge range were cut down? | The tiger and many other animals living there would lose their home and food | Tigers would just learn to live in the cities and eat from people's rubbish bins | Tigers would start eating grass | Nothing, tigers do not need forests
test: «tigers in China are fine, loads of them in the wild» What is true of the South China tiger? | None have been seen in the wild for decades; it lives only in zoos and breeding centres | Thousands roam the forests of China, more than in any other country in the world | It is the most common tiger in the world | It lives only in Africa
test: «hearing cranes but can't see 'em, they must be close» What is right? | Their trumpet calls can be heard several kilometres away, even when too high to see | Cranes are always silent in the air, so it must be some other kind of bird close by | Only a crane right next to you can be heard | You are hearing an aeroplane
test: «tiger numbers going up? news is fake, they're all gone» What is right? | Counts were reported to rise in India and Nepal, to around 5,500 worldwide | Yes, there are no wild tigers left anywhere on Earth, only the ones kept in zoos | There are millions of wild tigers | Tigers were never in danger
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the concealment afforded by the coloration of the tiger. | Deer are unable to distinguish orange from green, so that the coat appears to them as a dull green | The coat of the tiger alters to green during the hunt, in the manner of a chameleon | The prey animals of the tiger are without the faculty of sight | Concealment is obtained exclusively during the hours of darkness
exam: Identify the vocalisation by which tigers are observed to greet one another in an amicable manner. | A soft puffing sound designated as chuffing | Continuous purring after the manner of the domestic cat | A repeated barking sound | A prolonged and loud roar
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present status of the South China tiger? | It has not been observed in the wild for several decades and is maintained in captivity only | It is the most numerous tiger in the wild at the present time, inhabiting every province of China | It inhabits the forests of Africa | It has never existed save in works of art
exam: Ascertain the feature which most readily permits a crane in flight to be distinguished from a heron. | The crane extends its neck straight, whereas the heron folds its neck in the form of the letter S | The crane flies in an inverted position, its back turned towards the ground below | The heron possesses no neck of any length | Only the crane makes use of its wings in flight
